# Grade 4: teaching scripts, student steps and saint stories.
R = {
'4-1-1': dict(
  saint="Basil the Great: a bishop who defended the faith and fed the hungry.",
  tryit=["Put the twelve statements of the Apostles’ Creed in order.", "Say what each one means.", "Say the Creed together."],
  saintStory="Basil was born around the year 330 in Cappadocia, in what is now Turkey, to a family of saints: his grandmother, his parents, his sister Macrina and his brothers Gregory and Peter are all remembered as saints. He studied in Athens, where he became close friends with another future saint, Gregory of Nazianzus.\nBasil gave up a promising career to become a monk, and wrote rules to help monks live together. In 370 he became bishop of Caesarea. During a terrible famine he sold his own goods and gave out food himself. He built a large complex of buildings for the sick and poor, a hospital, that people called the Basiliad.\nBasil also defended the faith when many people denied that the Son and the Holy Spirit are truly God. He died in 379. Two years later, the Council of Constantinople finished the Creed we say at Mass on Sundays, using the ideas Basil had defended.",
  script=dict(
    open="Put on a team jersey or hold a flag. Ask, “What does this say about you? When you wear a team’s jersey, everyone knows what you believe in. Christians have a ‘jersey’ too, a short statement of what we believe. It is called the Creed.”",
    teach=["Say: “The word Creed comes from the Latin word credo, which means ‘I believe.’ The Creed is a short summary of what the Church believes, handed down from the apostles. St. Paul wrote, ‘If you confess with your lips that Jesus is Lord and believe in your heart that God raised him from the dead, you will be saved.’ (Romans 10:9) In 1 Corinthians 15:3-5, he gives an early summary: ‘Christ died for our sins… he was buried… he was raised on the third day.’”", "Say: “We use two Creeds. The Apostles’ Creed is the oldest, from the early baptismal questions of Rome. At Baptism, the priest asks, ‘Do you believe in God, the Father almighty?’ and we answer ‘I do.’ We pray it in the Rosary. The Nicene Creed was written by bishops at the Council of Nicaea in 325, and completed at the Council of Constantinople in 381. We say it on Sundays at Mass. These councils had to state clearly what the Church believes, because some were teaching that Jesus was not truly God.”", "Say: “The Apostles’ Creed has twelve statements, called articles. They are about God the Father and creation; Jesus; the Holy Spirit and the Church. When we say ‘I believe,’ we don’t only mean ‘I think this is true.’ We mean: ‘I trust God and give my life to him.’ We say ‘I believe’ at Baptism and at Mass. It is the way we say yes to God.”"],
    ask=["What does ‘Creed’ mean? (I believe.)", "When do we say the Nicene Creed? (At Mass on Sundays and holy days.)", "Why does the Church have a Creed? (To state clearly what we believe, handed down from the apostles.)"],
    practice=["Write the twelve articles of the Apostles’ Creed on strips of paper (one per strip).", "Divide into teams. Each team gets a mixed-up set and races to put them in order.", "Check against the text. For each article, a team member says, in plain words, what it means.", "Pray the Creed together slowly."],
    close="Pray the Apostles’ Creed together. Sign of the Cross. Send home: “Pray the Creed with your family, and listen for it at Mass.”")),

'4-1-2': dict(
  saint="Mary: the model of faith from Nazareth to the cross.",
  tryit=["Read Hebrews 11 and one story of faith.", "Make a poster of your hero of faith.", "Say what it cost them to trust God."],
  saintStory="Mary was a young girl from Nazareth, probably in her teens, when the angel Gabriel greeted her as “full of grace.” He told her she would conceive and bear a son, the Son of the Most High. She did not understand everything, and she asked, “How can this be?” But she said, “Let it be done to me according to your word.”\nHer faith was tested again and again. She gave birth in a stable. She fled to Egypt. She lost Jesus for three days in Jerusalem and did not understand his answer. She watched her Son be rejected and crucified. And she stood at the foot of the cross. The Gospels say that she “kept all these things in her heart.”\nElizabeth said to her, “Blessed is she who believed.” Mary shows us what faith is: trusting God even when we don’t see the whole picture.",
  script=dict(
    open="Hold up a pair of glasses and say, “If I take these off, I can’t see clearly. Faith is like a pair of glasses that helps us see what we cannot see with our eyes. Let’s find out what it is.”",
    teach=["Read Hebrews 11:1-3: “Faith is the assurance of things hoped for, the conviction of things not seen.” Say: “Faith means trusting God and believing everything he has revealed, because God cannot deceive or be deceived. We can’t see God, but we believe because he has told us. It is like trusting a friend who always tells the truth.”", "Say: “Faith is a gift and a choice. It is a gift because we can’t make ourselves have faith. It is God’s grace, given at Baptism, with the help of the Holy Spirit. But it is also a choice: God does not force us. We respond freely: ‘Yes, I believe.’ And faith is not against reason. God made our minds, and he gave us reasons to believe. But faith goes further than what we can prove.”", "Say: “Hebrews 11 lists heroes of faith. Abraham left home not knowing where he was going. Noah built an ark when there was no rain. Moses led the people through the sea. Mary said yes to the angel. Faith is not just believing facts, but acting on them. It is trusting enough to act. And faith grows when we use it, like a muscle. We pray, ‘Lord, increase my faith.’”"],
    ask=["What is faith? (Trusting God and believing what he has revealed.)", "Why can we trust God? (He cannot lie or be wrong.)", "Who are examples of faith? (Abraham, Noah, Moses, Mary.)"],
    practice=["Split into groups. Each group reads Hebrews 11:1-3 and then one story: Abraham (Genesis 12:1-4), Noah (Genesis 6:13-22), Moses (Exodus 14) or Mary (Luke 1:26-38).", "On a poster, they draw the person and write: What did God ask? What did they do? What did it cost? What happened?", "Post the posters, and do a gallery walk.", "Each child writes one sentence on what they learn about faith."],
    close="Pray: “Lord, I believe; help my unbelief.” (Mark 9:24) Sign of the Cross. Send home: “Tell someone about a time you had to trust God.”")),

'4-1-3': dict(
  saint="Gregory of Nazianzus: a bishop called “the Theologian” because he spoke so well about the Trinity.",
  tryit=["Look at the pictures that try to show the Trinity.", "Decide which are good and which mislead.", "Make a better one."],
  saintStory="Gregory was born around the year 329 in Cappadocia, and he studied in Athens with his friend Basil. He wanted to live a quiet life of prayer and writing, but his father, who was a bishop, and the Church needed him. In 380 the Christians in Constantinople asked him to come and lead them, in a city where many people had stopped believing that Jesus is truly God.\nHe preached powerful sermons in a small church, which he called the Anastasia, “the Resurrection.” In five famous talks, he explained the Trinity: that the Father, Son and Holy Spirit are three Persons, but one God. He said that the Father is the source, the Son is begotten, and the Spirit proceeds.\nThe Church calls him “the Theologian.” At the Council of Constantinople in 381, he helped to set the words of the Creed we pray today. Gregory shows us that the Trinity is not a puzzle to solve, but a mystery to love.",
  script=dict(
    open="Hold up three candles. Light them one at a time. Ask, “Is this one fire or three?” Let children think. Then hold them together. “The flames are three, but the light is one. It’s not a perfect picture, but it gets us started.”",
    teach=["Read Matthew 28:19. Say: “Jesus told the apostles to baptize ‘in the name of the Father and of the Son and of the Holy Spirit.’ Notice: it’s one ‘name,’ but three Persons. Paul ended a letter with a blessing: ‘The grace of the Lord Jesus Christ, and the love of God, and the fellowship of the Holy Spirit be with you all.’ (2 Corinthians 13:13) The Trinity is the central mystery of the faith.”", "Say: “There is one God in three Persons: Father, Son and Holy Spirit. The three Persons are distinct, which means they are not the same person. The Father is not the Son, and the Son is not the Spirit. But they are also equal: each is fully God, with the same power and love. And there are not three gods, but one God. This is a mystery: a truth so great that we cannot fully understand it, but we can know it is true because God has revealed it.”", "Say: “Every picture of the Trinity is incomplete. A shamrock suggests three leaves, but three leaves of one plant are parts, and each Person is fully God. Water that is ice, liquid and steam, but one substance at a time, not all at once. The sun with its light and heat, but those are less than the sun. These pictures can help a little, and each fails somewhere. The truth of the Trinity shows us that God is love: a relationship of giving and receiving love, within God himself. And God invites us into it.”"],
    ask=["How many Gods are there? How many Persons? (One God, three Persons.)", "Why does the shamrock picture not work perfectly? (The leaves are parts; each Person is fully God.)", "What does the Trinity show about God? (God is love, a communion of Persons.)"],
    practice=["Show three or four common pictures of the Trinity (shamrock, water in three states, sun and heat and light, an egg).", "In pairs, children decide for each: what does it help us see, and where does it fail?", "Share ideas in the whole group.", "Then try making a better picture or just a ‘statement’ of the faith in words: “The Father is God, the Son is God, the Holy Spirit is God, and they are one God.”"],
    close="Pray the Glory Be, slowly, together. Sign of the Cross. Send home: “Explain the Trinity to someone, using the Sign of the Cross.”")),

'4-2-1': dict(
  saint="Albert the Great: a scholar who studied nature and loved God.",
  tryit=["Make a seven-day poster for Genesis 1.", "Write what each day shows about God.", "Say thank you for each."],
  saintStory="Albert was born around the year 1200 in Germany. He joined the Dominicans and became a great teacher in Paris and Cologne. His most famous student was Thomas Aquinas, whom he met in Paris. Thomas was quiet and everyone called him the Dumb Ox, but Albert said that his bellowing would fill the world.\nAlbert was fascinated by nature. He studied plants, animals, rocks, stars and the weather, and wrote books about them. He did experiments and went out to observe things for himself. He believed that studying creation was a way to know the Creator. He knew that faith and reason do not conflict, because God is the author of both.\nHe was called “the Universal Doctor” because he knew so much. Albert is the patron saint of scientists. He shows that studying God’s world can be a way to praise him.",
  script=dict(
    open="Bring something natural: a leaf, a shell, a stone, a feather. Pass it around. Say, “Look at this carefully. Who made it? How was it made? Those are two different questions, and today we will see why both are good.”",
    teach=["Read Genesis 1:1-5 and part of Psalm 104. Say: “‘In the beginning, God created the heavens and the earth.’ God created everything out of nothing. Not from something else, not because he needed to, but freely and out of love. Everything God made is good. Over and over Genesis says, ‘And God saw that it was good.’ Then, when he made people, ‘it was very good.’”", "Say: “Creation has an order. Genesis 1 shows days: light, sky and sea, land and plants, sun and moon, fish and birds, animals and people, and rest. Each step prepares for the next. It teaches us that God is a God of order, and that he made everything for a purpose: to praise him and to serve us. God blessed the world, and then he rested on the seventh day, a holy day.”", "Say: “Science and faith ask different questions. Science asks how things work, and when. It studies the universe with experiments. Faith asks who made it, and why. Faith says that God is behind it all. Genesis is not a science textbook. It uses images and a story to teach that God made everything, that it is good, and that we are made in his image. The Church teaches that there is no real conflict between faith and science, because the same God is the author of both truth and creation.”"],
    ask=["What does it mean that God created ‘out of nothing’? (He didn’t need anything to make it.)", "What is the difference between how science and faith answer questions? (Science: how; faith: who and why.)", "Why is creation good? (God made it, and he is good.)"],
    practice=["Give each child a long sheet divided into seven columns.", "Under each day, children draw what God made, and write one thing it shows about God: his power, his love, his order, his creativity, his care.", "On the seventh day, draw how we rest and worship.", "Pair up and share your favorite day."],
    close="Pray Psalm 104:24: “O Lord, how manifold are your works!” Sign of the Cross. Send home: “Go outside, find something God made, and thank him.”")),

'4-2-2': dict(
  saint="Raphael the Archangel: an angel who guided a young traveler and healed.",
  tryit=["Sort the cards: angels, humans, both.", "Place them on the Venn diagram.", "Say what makes humans special."],
  saintStory="The Book of Tobit tells of a young man named Tobias who had to make a long journey. His father, Tobit, was old and blind. Tobias needed a guide, and a stranger offered to go with him. His name was Azariah, but he was really the archangel Raphael, which means “God heals.”\nOn the journey, Tobias was attacked by a big fish, and Raphael told him to take the fish’s heart, liver and gall, which would be used as medicine. Raphael led him safely to a distant city, helped him find a good wife named Sarah, who was in great trouble, and brought him home. There, he told Tobias to put the gall on his father’s eyes, and Tobit could see again.\nWhen the journey was over, Raphael told them who he was: “I am Raphael, one of the seven angels who stand before the Lord.” He tells us to thank God and praise him. Raphael shows how angels help us on the road.",
  script=dict(
    open="Turn off the lights and say, “Close your eyes. Imagine a bright being with great power, who has no body. What would you think?” Let children share. “Angels are real. Let’s see what the Bible says.”",
    teach=["Say: “Angels are pure spirits. They do not have bodies, as we do. God made them before the world and they serve him. They have minds and wills, they are very powerful, and they are always praising God. The word ‘angel’ means ‘messenger,’ which describes what they do for us. Psalm 148:2: ‘Praise him, all his angels.’ Three are named in the Bible: Michael, Gabriel and Raphael.”", "Say: “Angels are also our helpers. Every person has a guardian angel from the moment of birth, as Jesus teaches in Matthew 18:10. Angels protect us and guide us. They bring our prayers to God. But we should not worship angels. We worship only God, and we thank him for sending them.”", "Read Genesis 2:7: “Then the Lord God formed man of dust from the ground, and breathed into his nostrils the breath of life.” Say: “Humans are different. We are a union of body and soul. Our body is made of matter, and our soul is a spiritual part that God creates for each person. The soul never dies. We are made in God’s image, and called to know and love him. Angels are pure spirit. Animals have bodies and life but not an immortal soul. Humans are in the middle, matter and spirit together, which is why we are special.”"],
    ask=["What are angels? (Pure spirits who serve and praise God.)", "What is a guardian angel? (An angel God gives each person to protect and guide.)", "How are humans different from angels and from animals? (Body and soul; made in God’s image.)"],
    practice=["Draw a large Venn diagram with three areas: Angels, Humans, Both.", "Give out cards with traits: has a body, has a soul, is a pure spirit, praises God, can know and love, has a guardian angel, can sin, is made by God, will die, will rise.", "Children place each card in the right area and say why.", "Finish by saying the Guardian Angel prayer together."],
    close="Pray the Guardian Angel prayer together. Sign of the Cross. Send home: “Thank your guardian angel every night.”")),

'4-2-3': dict(
  saint="Irenaeus: a bishop who showed that Mary undid what Eve did.",
  tryit=["Tell the story of the fall in four scenes.", "Draw what changed in each.", "Find the first promise of hope."],
  saintStory="Irenaeus was born around the year 130 in Smyrna, and as a boy he had listened to Polycarp, the old bishop who had known the apostle John. He moved to Gaul (France) and became a priest in the city of Lyon. After the bishop was killed in a persecution, in 177, he was chosen to take his place.\nSome teachers were saying false things: that the world was evil, and that Jesus only seemed to be human. Irenaeus wrote a big book, Against Heresies, to answer them. He taught that God made the world good, that Jesus truly became man, and that he came to heal everything that was damaged by sin.\nHe wrote about Adam and Eve and about Mary, seeing that Eve’s “no” was undone by Mary’s “yes.” He is the one who wrote, “The glory of God is man fully alive.” Irenaeus helps us see the story of the fall, and of God’s promise.",
  script=dict(
    open="Show a beautiful vase and ask a child to hold it. Say, “If I dropped it, what would happen? Something beautiful would break. Today we hear about something even more precious that was broken, and how God planned to fix it.”",
    teach=["Say: “God made Adam and Eve good, and he put them in a garden with everything they needed. They were close to him. He gave them one rule: do not eat the fruit of one tree. A serpent, the devil, tempted Eve, ‘Did God really say that?’ He told her, ‘You will not die. You will be like God.’ They disobeyed. This is the Fall, also called original sin.”", "Say: “They lost the gift of friendship with God and the gifts that went with it. They felt shame and hid. Their relationship with each other was broken: Adam blamed Eve. They were sent out of the garden, and death entered the world. Original sin wounded human nature. Because of this first sin, all of us are born with a weakened nature, inclined to sin, and without the life of grace. We are not guilty of Adam’s personal sin, but we inherit its effects. That is why we need to be saved, and why we are baptized.”", "Say: “But God did not give up! Genesis 3:15 is the first announcement of good news. God said to the serpent, ‘I will put enmity between you and the woman, and between your offspring and hers; he shall bruise your head.’ It is called the ‘protoevangelium,’ the first gospel. A descendant of the woman will defeat the serpent. We know that is Jesus, with Mary beside him. The story of the Fall is sad, but it ends with a promise.”"],
    ask=["What was the first sin? (Adam and Eve disobeyed God.)", "What are the results of original sin? (A wounded nature, suffering, death, the tendency to sin.)", "What did God promise right away? (A Savior who would defeat the serpent.)"],
    practice=["Read Genesis 3 in four parts: the temptation, the sin, the hiding, the promise.", "After each part, children draw a quick picture on a four-panel sheet, and write a word for what changed: trust, shame, blame, hope.", "Underline the promise in Genesis 3:15 together, and trace it to Jesus and Mary.", "Share a word or phrase from their panels."],
    close="Pray: “Thank you, God, for the promise of a Savior.” Sign of the Cross. Send home: “Tell your family: sin is real, but God’s promise is stronger.”")),

'4-3-1': dict(
  saint="Anselm: a bishop who asked, “Why did God become man?”",
  tryit=["Act out the Annunciation.", "Freeze the scene.", "Step in as Mary and say what she felt."],
  saintStory="Anselm was born in 1033 in northern Italy, and as a young man he left home to study and became a monk at a monastery in Normandy. He loved to think about God. He became abbot and, later, Archbishop of Canterbury in England.\nHis motto was “faith seeking understanding.” He did not want to prove God to make himself believe. He wanted to understand better what he already loved. He wrote a book called “Why God Became Man” (Cur Deus Homo), in which he explained that only someone who is both God and man could repair the damage done by sin.\nAnselm quarreled with two English kings who tried to control the Church, and he was sent into exile twice, but he never gave up. He shows how the Incarnation is the greatest event in history, and worth thinking about deeply.",
  script=dict(
    open="Show a large paper with the word “yes” written on it. Say, “Can one word change history? There was a time when one young woman said one word, and it changed everything.”",
    teach=["Read Luke 1:26-38 aloud. Say: “In the sixth month, the angel Gabriel was sent by God to a town in Galilee called Nazareth, to a virgin named Mary, who was engaged to Joseph. He said, ‘Hail, full of grace, the Lord is with you.’ Mary was troubled, and the angel said, ‘Do not be afraid. You will conceive and bear a son, and you shall call his name Jesus. He will be great and will be called the Son of the Most High.’ Mary asked, ‘How can this be, since I have no husband?’ The angel said, ‘The Holy Spirit will come upon you.’ Mary said, ‘Behold, I am the servant of the Lord; let it be to me according to your word.’”", "Say: “This is the Incarnation: the Son of God took a human nature in Mary’s womb. We say in the Creed, ‘He was conceived by the Holy Spirit, born of the Virgin Mary.’ Jesus has no human father. His conception is a miracle, the work of the Holy Spirit. Mary stayed a virgin. This is the greatest event in history. The Son of God, who had no beginning, became a human being, while still being God. Mary’s ‘yes’ was free, and it made it possible.”", "Say: “Why does it matter? Because it shows how much God loves us. He came into our world as one of us, to teach, to heal, and to save. ‘The Word became flesh and dwelt among us.’ (John 1:14) We celebrate the Annunciation on March 25, nine months before Christmas, and we pray the Angelus every day at morning, noon and evening to remember it.”"],
    ask=["What is the Incarnation? (God’s Son took a human nature in Mary’s womb.)", "Who is Jesus’ father? (God the Father; Jesus has no earthly father by nature; Joseph was his foster father.)", "What did Mary say to the angel? (Let it be done to me according to your word.)"],
    practice=["Choose volunteers to be Gabriel, Mary and narrators. Act out Luke 1:26-38 slowly, with simple movements.", "Freeze the scene at Mary’s answer. Tap the shoulder of other children one at a time to step into Mary’s place and say aloud what she might have been thinking.", "Discuss how it felt to say yes.", "Pray the Angelus together, or the Hail Mary."],
    close="Pray the Angelus or the Hail Mary. Sign of the Cross. Send home: “Pray the Angelus at noon with someone.”")),

'4-3-2': dict(
  saint="Simon of Cyrene: a stranger who carried Jesus’ cross.",
  tryit=["Make a timeline from Palm Sunday to Easter.", "Add one event and one sign each day.", "Point out where Simon helped."],
  saintStory="The Gospels mention Simon in a few lines. He was a man from Cyrene, in North Africa, who had come to Jerusalem, probably for the Passover. As he was coming in from the countryside, the Roman soldiers grabbed him and forced him to carry the cross of Jesus, who was too weak to carry it by himself after being scourged.\nWe do not know what Simon thought. He may have been angry at first, because it was not his choice. But he walked behind Jesus all the way to the place called Golgotha. Mark’s Gospel describes him as “the father of Alexander and Rufus,” which suggests that his sons were known to the first Christian readers, and that Simon’s family became Christians.\nSimon shows us what Jesus said: “Whoever wishes to come after me must take up his cross and follow me.” Sometimes we help carry the cross without choosing it.",
  script=dict(
    open="Say, “Imagine a week. On Sunday, people shout and cheer. By Friday, a few of the same people are shouting something else. What could happen in one week to change a city?” Let children guess. “This is the story of Holy Week.”",
    teach=["Tell the days: “Palm Sunday: Jesus enters Jerusalem on a donkey, and people shout ‘Hosanna!’ and lay down palm branches. Holy Thursday: At the Last Supper, he gives us the Eucharist and washes the feet of his disciples. Then he prays in the Garden of Gethsemane, and Judas hands him over. Good Friday: Jesus is tried, condemned by Pilate, scourged, crowned with thorns and made to carry the cross. He is crucified at Golgotha.”", "Say: “Read John 19:30. ‘It is finished.’ Jesus gave his life freely to forgive our sins. He died, and was buried. The Creed says: ‘Suffered under Pontius Pilate, was crucified, died and was buried.’ The Romans were responsible for killing Jesus, but all of us sinners are the reason he died. Jesus did it because he loves us. ‘Greater love has no one than this, that someone lay down his life for his friends.’”", "Say: “Then, on the third day, Easter Sunday, Jesus rose. ‘He is not here, for he has risen.’ (Luke 24:6) The Resurrection is the foundation of our faith. It shows that Jesus is God, that sin and death are defeated, and that we will rise. Jesus did not just come back to his old life. He rose with a glorified body, never to die again. He appeared to Mary Magdalene, Peter, the disciples, and over five hundred people. Easter is the greatest feast of the Church year.”"],
    ask=["Why did Jesus die? (To forgive our sins, because he loves us.)", "What does the Resurrection show? (Jesus is God, and death and sin are defeated.)", "What is different about Jesus’ risen body? (It is glorified and will never die.)"],
    practice=["Give each child a long strip of paper, folded into seven or eight parts: Palm Sunday, Holy Thursday, Good Friday, Holy Saturday, Easter Sunday.", "For each day, write one event and draw one sign (a palm branch, bread and cup, a cross, a tomb, a lamp).", "Add a small note about where Simon of Cyrene appears.", "Tell the week to a partner using the strip."],
    close="Pray: “Jesus, you died and rose for us. Thank you.” Sign of the Cross. Send home: “Retell Holy Week with your family.”")),

'4-3-3': dict(
  saint="Bede the Venerable: a monk who worked until his last day.",
  tryit=["List what Jesus has already done.", "List what we are still waiting for.", "Say: “Come, Lord Jesus.”"],
  saintStory="Bede was born around 673 in the north of England, and when he was seven his family sent him to the monastery of Wearmouth, and later to Jarrow. He lived there for the rest of his life. He loved to study, to teach and to write. He wrote on the Bible, on nature and time, and about the history of England, The Ecclesiastical History of the English People. He is sometimes called the “Father of English history.”\nBede was a gentle man who sang the psalms with his brothers every day. When he was dying, in 735, he was still working, dictating a translation of the Gospel of John into English to a young helper. The helper said, “There is only one sentence left.” Bede said, “Write it quickly.” When it was finished, he said, “It is finished,” and died, praying the Glory Be, around the feast of the Ascension.\nHe is the only English person named a Doctor of the Church. Bede shows us how to live looking toward Jesus’ return, working to the end, and trusting him.",
  script=dict(
    open="Hold a sign that says “Already” on one side and “Not yet” on the other. Ask, “If you plant a seed, the plant is ‘already’ there in a way, but ‘not yet’ grown. Today we talk about two things Jesus has done, and one he will do.”",
    teach=["Read Acts 1:6-11. Say: “Forty days after Easter, Jesus appeared to his disciples for the last time. He told them to wait for the Holy Spirit and be his witnesses ‘to the end of the earth.’ Then, as they watched, he was lifted up, and a cloud took him out of their sight. Two men in white said, ‘Why do you stand looking into heaven? This Jesus will come in the same way as you saw him go.’ This is the Ascension.”", "Say: “The Creed says: ‘He ascended into heaven and is seated at the right hand of the Father.’ The right hand of the Father is a way of saying that Jesus has the place of honor and power. Jesus is not far away. He reigns now as King and Lord. He is also close to us in his Church: in the sacraments, in the Bible, in each other, and in the poor. He prays for us. And he has prepared a place for us.”", "Say: “The Creed goes on: ‘He will come again in glory to judge the living and the dead, and his kingdom will have no end.’ This is the Second Coming. Nobody knows when. Jesus will come back at the end of time, to make everything right. We live ‘already’ in his kingdom, and we wait for it to be complete. So we pray, ‘Come, Lord Jesus.’ And we try to be ready. Jesus will judge everyone with perfect justice and mercy.”"],
    ask=["What does ‘seated at the right hand of the Father’ mean? (Jesus has the place of honor and power.)", "Where is Jesus now? (In heaven, and also with us through his Church.)", "What will Jesus do when he comes again? (Judge the living and the dead, and complete his kingdom.)"],
    practice=["Make a two-column chart: “Jesus has already…” and “Jesus will…”", "In the first column: was born, taught, died, rose, ascended, sent the Holy Spirit, gave us the Church. In the second: come again in glory, judge, bring a new heaven and a new earth.", "Add what we do while we wait: pray, serve, tell others.", "Share and pray together “Come, Lord Jesus.”"],
    close="Pray: “Come, Lord Jesus.” Sign of the Cross. Send home: “Ask your family what you can do to be ready for Jesus.”")),

'4-4-1': dict(
  saint="Catherine of Siena: a young woman filled with the Spirit who spoke boldly to popes.",
  tryit=["Visit the Spirit’s symbols: water, oil, fire, wind, dove.", "Say what each one shows.", "Draw your favorite."],
  saintStory="Catherine was born in 1347 in Siena, Italy, the twenty-third of twenty-five children. When she was about six, she had a vision of Jesus, and she decided to give her life to him. Her parents wanted her to marry, but she cut her hair and refused. She became a Dominican tertiary, which means she lived in the world, in her family home, with a rule of prayer.\nCatherine spent her time in prayer and caring for the sick, especially people with plague. She couldn’t write at first, but she dictated hundreds of letters to leaders, even to kings and popes. At the time, the popes were living in Avignon, France, and Catherine wrote to Pope Gregory XI to return to Rome. In 1377 he did.\nShe died in Rome in 1380, at thirty-three. She is a Doctor of the Church. Catherine shows how the Holy Spirit gives courage and wisdom to ordinary people.",
  script=dict(
    open="Blow out a candle, or use a pinwheel with a fan. Ask, “What made the wind move? We can’t see it, but we see what it does. The Holy Spirit is like that. Today we will meet the Spirit.”",
    teach=["Say: “The Holy Spirit is the third Person of the Trinity. The Nicene Creed calls him ‘the Lord, the giver of life, who proceeds from the Father and the Son, who with the Father and the Son is adored and glorified.’ He is God, equal to the Father and the Son. We cannot see him, but he is at work. He inspired the prophets and the writers of Scripture. He overshadowed Mary. He came upon Jesus at his baptism. He came upon the apostles at Pentecost.”", "Read John 16:13: “When the Spirit of truth comes, he will guide you into all the truth.” Say: “Jesus promised to send the Holy Spirit to teach and guide us. The Spirit lives in the Church and in each baptized person. He gives us life, helps us pray, tells us what is true, gives us gifts and makes us holy. He also makes the Church one.”", "Say: “The Bible uses symbols to show what the Spirit does. Water: he gives new life, as in Baptism. Oil: he anoints and strengthens us, as in Confirmation. Fire: he warms, cleanses and gives courage, as at Pentecost. Wind or breath: he is the breath of God’s life. A dove: peace, as at Jesus’ baptism. A cloud and light: God’s presence. Each tells us something about how the Spirit works.”"],
    ask=["Who is the Holy Spirit? (The third Person of the Trinity, God.)", "What did the Spirit do at Pentecost? (Came to the apostles and the Church began.)", "What are some symbols of the Spirit? (Water, oil, fire, wind, dove.)"],
    practice=["Set up five stations: water (a bowl to touch), oil (a scented oil to smell), fire (a candle, watched from a distance), wind (a fan or paper to blow), dove (a picture).", "At each, children read a short Scripture verse and write what the symbol shows about the Spirit.", "Rotate every four minutes.", "Each child picks one symbol and draws it with a sentence."],
    close="Pray the “Come, Holy Spirit” prayer. Sign of the Cross. Send home: “Ask the Holy Spirit for help every morning.”")),

'4-4-2': dict(
  saint="Katharine Drexel: a rich young woman who gave everything to serve others.",
  tryit=["Draw a big body.", "Label each part with a person in your parish.", "Say how each one is needed."],
  saintStory="Katharine Drexel was born in 1858 in Philadelphia, into one of the richest families in the United States. Her father was a banker. She grew up with servants and travel, but her parents also gave to the poor, and her stepmother opened their home to the needy twice a week.\nWhen she traveled out west, she saw the poverty of Native Americans and African Americans, who had few schools. When she met the Pope and asked him to send missionaries, he suggested, “Why don’t you become a missionary yourself?” She did. She gave away her inheritance and, at thirty-three, founded the Sisters of the Blessed Sacrament.\nKatharine’s sisters opened dozens of schools and missions across the country, including Xavier University in New Orleans, the first Catholic university for African Americans. She lived to be ninety-six. She shows that everyone in the Body of Christ has a part to play.",
  script=dict(
    open="Show a picture of a human body, and ask, “If one part says to another, ‘I don’t need you,’ what would happen?” Let children share ideas. “St. Paul used this picture to describe the Church.”",
    teach=["Read 1 Corinthians 12:12-27 in summary. Say: “Paul says: ‘Just as the body is one and has many members, so it is with Christ.’ The Church is the Body of Christ. Jesus is the head, and we are the members, the parts. Each part has a different job. The eye cannot say to the hand, ‘I don’t need you.’ If one part suffers, all suffer. If one is honored, all rejoice.”", "Say: “This means that everyone in the Church is needed. Not just the priest or the Pope. Some are teachers, some are singers, some are helpers, some pray, some work with the poor. Each has gifts from the Holy Spirit, and each gift is for the good of all. We are baptized into one body, and we belong to each other.”", "Say: “Jesus is the head, so he gives the body life and direction. The Holy Spirit is the soul of the body, who gives life to all members. When we receive Communion, we become more united with Jesus and with one another. When one of us sins, it harms the whole body. And when we do good, it strengthens everyone.”"],
    ask=["Who is the head of the Church? (Jesus.)", "What does it mean that we are members of one body? (We all belong and we all need each other.)", "What gift do you bring to the body? (Take answers.)"],
    practice=["Draw a large human body on a poster.", "Label the parts with real roles in your parish: eyes (lectors who see and read God’s word), mouth (cantors and preachers), hands (those who serve and help), feet (those who visit the sick, missionaries), ears (listeners), heart (those who pray).", "Write names of real people next to each role, and add yourself.", "Discuss: what happens if one part is missing?"],
    close="Pray: “Jesus, help us work together as one body.” Sign of the Cross. Send home: “Thank someone in our parish for their part.”")),

'4-4-3': dict(
  saint="Peter Canisius: a teacher who explained the Church’s faith to many people.",
  tryit=["Go on a scavenger hunt for the four marks.", "Find examples of one, holy, catholic and apostolic.", "Write or photograph what you find."],
  saintStory="Peter Canisius was born in 1521 in the Netherlands. He studied in Cologne and became a Jesuit. He lived at a time of confusion, when many Catholics in Germany were leaving the Church, and many were uncertain what the Church taught.\nPeter traveled on foot or by horseback across Germany, Austria, Bohemia and Switzerland, preaching, teaching and starting schools. He wrote catechisms: books to explain the faith in simple questions and answers. His small catechism was used for hundreds of years, and he wrote for different ages.\nHe is called “the second apostle of Germany.” He died in 1597. Peter Canisius shows that the Church’s marks of unity, holiness, universality and apostolic roots are real, and that teaching them is a loving work.",
  script=dict(
    open="Show a picture of a church building, and a picture of a different building. Ask, “How can you tell which is a church? What are the signs?” Take answers. “How can you tell which Church is the one Jesus founded? It, too, has signs, called marks.”",
    teach=["Say: “We say in the Creed: ‘I believe in one, holy, catholic and apostolic Church.’ These are the four marks of the Church. They come from Christ and the Holy Spirit, and they help us recognize the Church Jesus founded. In Matthew 16:18, Jesus said to Peter, ‘You are Peter, and on this rock I will build my Church, and the gates of hell shall not prevail against it.’”", "Say: “One: the Church is one because she has one Lord, one faith, one Baptism, and one leader after Peter, the Pope. In Ephesians 4:4-6, Paul says, ‘one body and one Spirit… one Lord, one faith, one baptism.’ Holy: the Church is holy because Christ, her head, is holy, and gives her the sacraments, the Gospel and the Holy Spirit. Her members sin, but she is holy in what she gives us. Catholic means ‘universal.’ The Church is for all people and all times. She has the fullness of faith and the means of salvation. Apostolic: she is built on the apostles and still taught by their successors, the bishops, in an unbroken line from them.”", "Say: “These marks are not just signs for others. They are also a call for us. We should work for unity, strive for holiness, share the faith with everyone, and stay faithful to what the apostles taught. When the Church sees her members fail, she turns to Christ to be renewed. And the marks remind us that Jesus’ promise holds: the gates of hell will not prevail.”"],
    ask=["Name the four marks. (One, holy, catholic, apostolic.)", "What does ‘catholic’ mean? (Universal.)", "What does ‘apostolic’ mean? (Built on the apostles; led by their successors.)"],
    practice=["Go around the parish, or look at photographs, to find examples of each mark: one (the same Mass in every church; a picture of the Pope), holy (the sacraments, saints’ statues), catholic (people of different nationalities, a world map), apostolic (the bishop’s name in the Mass, a list of Popes).", "Take notes or photos in teams.", "Each team presents what it found.", "Add a sentence: how can I live this mark?"],
    close="Pray the line from the Creed: “I believe in one, holy, catholic and apostolic Church.” Sign of the Cross. Send home: “Look for the four marks this week.”")),

'4-5-1': dict(
  saint="Dominic: he promised to help his friends from heaven.",
  tryit=["Write a line for a litany of saints.", "Choose a saint and what they teach us.", "Pray it together."],
  saintStory="Dominic de Guzmán was born in Spain around 1170. He became a priest and, as he traveled, he saw that many people did not know the faith, so he began a new order, the Order of Preachers, now called the Dominicans, to teach and preach. He gathered a group of friars who would study, pray and travel on foot.\nDominic was a joyful, gentle man, who loved to pray at night. He taught people to pray the Rosary, according to tradition. By the time he was fifty-one, his friars had spread across Europe. He became ill on a trip to Bologna, in Italy, and his friars gathered round him. He told them, “Do not weep; I shall be more useful to you after my death, and I shall help you then more effectively than during my life.”\nHe died on August 6, 1221. Dominic’s promise is what the communion of saints means: those in heaven help those on earth.",
  script=dict(
    open="Show a photo of a family with grandparents who have passed away, or a family tree. Ask, “Are they still part of the family, even though they’re not here? The Church is a family that includes those who’ve died.”",
    teach=["Say: “The Apostles’ Creed says, ‘I believe in the communion of saints.’ It means all those who belong to Christ are connected in one big family. There are three groups: the Church on earth, the pilgrim Church, still on the journey. The Church in purgatory, the souls who have died in God’s friendship and are being made ready for heaven. And the Church in heaven, the saints, who see God face to face. Death does not break the bonds of love.”", "Read Hebrews 12:1-2: “Therefore, since we are surrounded by so great a cloud of witnesses… let us run with perseverance the race that is set before us, looking to Jesus.” Say: “The saints are like a stadium full of people cheering us on. They pray for us, and we can ask them to pray for us, just as we ask friends. They are not rivals to Jesus. They bring us to him.”", "Say: “The communion of saints also means that we share holy things: the sacraments, the prayers and the merits of Christ. We can pray for the souls in purgatory, especially at Mass. We pray for the whole Church. And we honor the saints by imitating their lives. A litany is a prayer that lists the saints one at a time, with the response, ‘pray for us.’”"],
    ask=["What are the three parts of the communion of saints? (Church on earth, in purgatory, in heaven.)", "How do the saints help us? (They pray for us and show us how to live.)", "How can we help the souls in purgatory? (Pray for them, offer Mass.)"],
    practice=["Each child chooses a saint they like.", "On a card, they write a line for a class litany: “St. ___, teach us to ___.” For example: “St. Francis, teach us to love creation.”", "Gather in a circle. One child reads each line, and everyone responds, “Pray for us.”", "Add the Guardian Angel prayer."],
    close="Pray the litany you wrote together. Sign of the Cross. Send home: “Pray for a family member who has died.”")),

'4-5-2': dict(
  saint="Alphonsus Liguori: a gentle confessor and the patron of confessors.",
  tryit=["Put the steps of Confession in order.", "Say what happens in each.", "Practice the Act of Contrition."],
  saintStory="Alphonsus was born near Naples, Italy, in 1696. He was very smart and became a lawyer at sixteen. But in 1723 he lost an important case, because he had missed a key paper, and he was so ashamed that he gave up law and decided to become a priest.\nHe worked among the poor, in the slums of Naples, and in the countryside, where he found that most people were uneducated about faith and had no one to teach them. He founded a congregation, the Redemptorists, to preach to the poor. He also wrote books, including a guide for confessors that helped them be kind and wise, and not harsh. He wanted people to feel welcome.\nAlphonsus became a bishop and lived to ninety-one. He is the patron of confessors. He shows that Confession is a gift of God’s mercy, and priests should offer it with gentleness.",
  script=dict(
    open="Put a puddle of muddy water on a sheet, and a bucket of clean water. Ask, “Is this clean? How do we get the mud out?” Say, “When we sin, our soul gets muddy. God gave us a way to clean it.”",
    teach=["Say: “In the Creed we say, ‘I believe in the forgiveness of sins.’ God forgives our sins in two main ways. At Baptism, original sin and all personal sins are washed away. But after Baptism, we can sin again. So Jesus gave us another sacrament: Reconciliation, also called Confession or Penance. On Easter night, Jesus breathed on the apostles and said, ‘Receive the Holy Spirit. If you forgive the sins of any, they are forgiven.’ (John 20:21-23) The apostles passed this power on to bishops and priests.”", "Say: “There are five steps: One: Examine your conscience: think about your sins, with the Ten Commandments or a guide. Two: Be sorry for your sins. This is called contrition. Three: Confess your sins to the priest, honestly and completely. Four: Receive a penance from the priest: a prayer or good act to repair the harm and begin again. Five: Receive absolution, when the priest says, ‘I absolve you from your sins in the name of the Father, and of the Son, and of the Holy Spirit.’ Then we promise to try not to sin again, and we give thanks.”", "Say: “The priest can never reveal anything that is said in Confession. This is called the seal of Confession. Sometimes we feel afraid or embarrassed. But the priest is there in the person of Christ, to forgive, to guide and to help. Many people feel lighter and happier afterward. God never tires of forgiving us.”"],
    ask=["Who gave the apostles power to forgive sins? (Jesus.)", "What are the steps of Confession? (Examine, be sorry, confess, penance, absolution.)", "What is the seal of Confession? (The priest can never tell what he hears.)"],
    practice=["Write the five steps on cards: examine your conscience, be sorry, confess, receive penance, absolution.", "Mix them up. In teams, put them in order, and say what happens at each.", "Practice the Act of Contrition aloud, line by line.", "Quietly think about one thing you want to be sorry for (without telling anyone)."],
    close="Pray the Act of Contrition together. Sign of the Cross. Send home: “Plan a time for Confession with your family.”")),

'4-5-3': dict(
  saint="Lazarus: the friend of Jesus whom he raised from the dead.",
  tryit=["List what Scripture says about heaven.", "Sort it into: what we know and what we do not know.", "Describe what you hope for."],
  saintStory="Lazarus lived in Bethany, a village near Jerusalem, with his sisters Martha and Mary. Jesus often stayed with them, and the Gospel says that Jesus loved them. When Lazarus became sick, his sisters sent word to Jesus, but by the time he arrived, Lazarus had been dead for four days.\nMartha went out to meet Jesus and said, “If you had been here, my brother would not have died.” Jesus told her, “I am the resurrection and the life. Whoever believes in me, though he die, yet shall he live.” When Jesus saw Mary and the others crying, he wept. Then he went to the tomb, and said, “Lazarus, come out!” And the man who had died walked out, still wrapped in burial cloths.\nTradition says that Lazarus later went to the island of Cyprus and became a bishop. Lazarus’ story points to what will happen to us, and it shows that Jesus is stronger than death.",
  script=dict(
    open="Say, “Close your eyes. Think about a person or pet you loved who has died. It is okay to feel sad. Jesus felt sad too. Today we learn what he promised us.”",
    teach=["Say: “The last words of the Creed are: ‘I believe in the resurrection of the body and life everlasting.’ This means that when we die, our soul goes to God to be judged. But at the end of time, Christ will come, and our bodies will be raised and joined again to our souls. We will rise, as Jesus did. Paul explains this in 1 Corinthians 15: ‘For as in Adam all die, so also in Christ shall all be made alive.’ Our new bodies will be transformed: glorious, strong, not subject to death.”", "Say: “What is heaven like? Scripture uses images: a wedding feast, a city of gold, a garden, light, a home. Revelation 21:4 says: ‘He will wipe away every tear from their eyes, and death shall be no more.’ But the main point is that we will be with God, and see him face to face. It is perfect happiness, with no sadness, sin or fear. We will see Mary, the angels, the saints and those we love.”", "Say: “Not everyone chooses God. Hell is the state of those who refuse God’s love to the end. God does not send anyone to hell; people choose it by refusing him. Purgatory is the cleansing of those who die in God’s friendship but need purification before heaven. All who die in God’s grace are sure of heaven. Our choices matter, and we live in hope and trust in God’s mercy.”"],
    ask=["What do we believe will happen at the end? (Our bodies will rise and join our souls.)", "What is heaven? (Life with God forever, seeing him face to face.)", "What do we hope for? (To be with God, with all those we love.)"],
    practice=["Read Revelation 21:1-5 and 1 Corinthians 15:20-26 aloud.", "Make two lists: “What the Bible tells us about heaven” (no more tears, God with us, new creation, no death, a home) and “What we still don’t know.”", "Talk about how it feels to hope.", "Draw or write a short description of what you look forward to most."],
    close="Pray: “Eternal rest grant unto them, O Lord, and let perpetual light shine upon them.” Sign of the Cross. Send home: “Pray for someone who has died.”")),

'4-6-1': dict(
  saint="John Duns Scotus: a Franciscan who explained why Mary could be preserved from sin.",
  tryit=["Read “full of grace” in different translations.", "Say what the angel’s greeting shows.", "Say the Hail Mary."],
  saintStory="John Duns was born around 1266 in Scotland, in a place called Duns. He became a Franciscan friar and studied and taught at Oxford and Paris. He was so clever with arguments that he was called “the Subtle Doctor.”\nHe wrote about many things, but he is especially remembered for his answer to a hard question. People wondered how Mary could be free from original sin, since Jesus died to save everyone. Scotus said that Jesus could save Mary in an even greater way: by preventing her from being stained, in view of his coming death. As he wrote, “God could do it, it was fitting that he did, therefore he did it.”\nPope John Paul II declared him Blessed in 1993. Scotus helped the Church understand the Immaculate Conception.",
  script=dict(
    open="Put out a spotless white cloth. Say, “Imagine God is going to be born on earth, and he needs a mother. Would he choose a mother with a spotless heart? What would he do to prepare her?”",
    teach=["Read Luke 1:28 in a few translations: “Hail, full of grace” (Douay), “Greetings, favored one” (RSV), “Rejoice, highly favored one.” Say: “The angel doesn’t say ‘Mary.’ He calls her ‘full of grace,’ as if it were her name. The Greek word suggests that she has been filled with grace and remains so. The Church sees that she is completely filled with God’s favor.”", "Say: “The Immaculate Conception is a special teaching. It means that from the first moment of her existence, Mary was kept free from original sin by a special gift from God, because she was to be the Mother of the Savior. ‘Immaculate’ means ‘without stain.’ It does not mean that Mary was conceived without a human father. It does not mean that she did not need a Savior. She was saved by Jesus’ death on the cross, in advance, in a special way. It is like being kept from falling into a pit, instead of being pulled out of one.”", "Say: “Pope Pius IX declared this teaching in 1854. Four years later, at Lourdes, Mary told Bernadette, ‘I am the Immaculate Conception.’ We celebrate the feast on December 8. This shows us God’s great power to save, and the beauty of what he wants to do in each of us. Mary is the first and best example of what God’s grace can do.”"],
    ask=["What does ‘Immaculate’ mean? (Without stain.)", "What did the angel call Mary? (Full of grace.)", "Did Mary need a Savior? (Yes; she was saved by Jesus in advance.)"],
    practice=["Write Luke 1:28 from three Bible translations on the board, and read them.", "Discuss why “full of grace” is almost a name, not just a greeting.", "In pairs, write a sentence about why God would prepare Mary this way.", "Pray the Hail Mary slowly, thinking about the words “full of grace.”"],
    close="Pray the Hail Mary together. Sign of the Cross. Send home: “Pray ‘O Mary, conceived without sin, pray for us.’”")),

'4-6-2': dict(
  tryit=["Act out the Council of Ephesus.", "Argue both sides.", "Say what is at stake."],
  saintStory="Cyril was born in Egypt around 376, and became the patriarch of Alexandria in 412. He was a tireless defender of the faith. When Nestorius, the patriarch of Constantinople, taught that Mary should only be called Mother of Christ, but not Mother of God, Cyril wrote to him and then to the Pope in Rome.\nThe issue was bigger than a title. If Mary is not Mother of God, then Jesus is not truly God and man in one person. A council of bishops met in the city of Ephesus in 431. Cyril presided. They decided that Mary is rightly called Theotokos, “God-bearer,” and that Jesus is one Person, truly God and truly human. When the news reached the people of Ephesus, they celebrated in the streets with torches.\nCyril’s work protected the truth about Jesus. His story shows why Mary’s titles matter: they protect the truth about her Son.",
  script=dict(
    open="Write two titles on the board: “Mother of Jesus’ human nature” and “Mother of God.” Ask, “Is there a difference?” Say, “In the year 431, the whole Church had to decide. We will have our own council.”",
    teach=["Say: “Elizabeth said to Mary, ‘Why is this granted me, that the mother of my Lord should come to me?’ (Luke 1:43) Paul said, ‘When the fullness of time had come, God sent forth his Son, born of woman.’ (Galatians 4:4) Mary is the mother of Jesus. And Jesus is God. So Mary is the Mother of God. This doesn’t mean she gave Jesus his divine nature, which always was. It means that the Person she bore is God.”", "Say: “Think of your own mother. She is not just the mother of your body. She is the mother of you, a whole person. In the same way, Mary is the mother of the whole Person of Jesus, who is God the Son in human flesh. If you say she is only mother of his human nature, then you are splitting Jesus into two, and that is false.”", "Say: “In 431, the Council of Ephesus defined this teaching, using the Greek title ‘Theotokos’: God-bearer. This protected the truth that Jesus is one Person, God and man. When we honor Mary as Mother of God, we are really honoring Jesus. And she is our mother too.”"],
    ask=["Why is Mary called Mother of God? (Because her Son, Jesus, is God.)", "What does ‘Theotokos’ mean? (God-bearer.)", "What does this title protect? (The truth that Jesus is God and man in one Person.)"],
    practice=["Set up a roleplay of the Council of Ephesus. Half the class are bishops who say Mary is only the mother of Jesus’ human nature, and half say she is the Mother of God.", "Each side prepares arguments for ten minutes, using the readings.", "Hold the debate, with a moderator. At the end, the “council” votes, and the teacher explains the Church’s decision.", "Reflect: why did the title matter?"],
    close="Pray the Hail Mary, especially: “Holy Mary, Mother of God, pray for us sinners.” Sign of the Cross. Send home: “Explain why Mary is the Mother of God.”")),

'4-6-3': dict(
  saint="John of Damascus: a monk who preached about Mary’s falling asleep and being taken to heaven.",
  tryit=["Make a calendar of Mary’s feasts.", "Add one fact for each.", "Mark the Assumption."],
  saintStory="John was born in Damascus, Syria, around the year 675, to a family of Christians who worked for the Muslim rulers. He was an official at the court, but he left his post and became a monk at the monastery of Mar Saba, near Jerusalem.\nJohn wrote about the faith for the whole Church. His great book, The Fount of Knowledge, collected the teaching of the Fathers. He defended the use of holy images, saying that since God became visible in Jesus, we can honor sacred pictures. He also wrote beautiful hymns, and homilies on the falling asleep of Mary.\nIn his sermons, he says that Mary was taken to heaven, that her body did not see corruption, and she was made Queen. John shows how the Church from the early centuries believed in the Assumption.",
  script=dict(
    open="Show a picture of the Assumption of Mary, and ask, “What is happening? Where is she going? What do you think this means?” Let children share. “This is one of the great feasts of the Church.”",
    teach=["Say: “At the end of her life on earth, Mary was taken body and soul into heavenly glory. This is the Assumption, which we celebrate on August 15. The Church has believed this from the early centuries, and in 1950 Pope Pius XII declared it a dogma: a truth that must be believed. The Bible doesn’t describe it, but it shows Mary’s role in Jesus’ plan, and the book of Revelation, chapter 12, describes ‘a woman clothed with the sun, with the moon under her feet, and on her head a crown of twelve stars.’”", "Say: “Why do we believe it? Because Jesus is the Savior, and he would not let the body that carried him decay. Mary shared fully in his Resurrection. She is the first to share in what Jesus promised to all of us at the end of time: that our bodies will rise. The Assumption shows what awaits us. It gives us hope.”", "Say: “Mary is also called Queen of Heaven. This is because her Son is the King of kings, in the line of David. In the royal family of Israel, the mother of the king was the queen mother, and she interceded for people. We honor Mary as queen, and we ask her to pray for us. We often pray the Hail, Holy Queen after the Rosary. Mary’s feasts give us a year of times to honor her.”"],
    ask=["What happened to Mary at the end of her life? (She was taken body and soul into heaven.)", "Why do we believe in the Assumption? (She shared fully in Jesus’ Resurrection; the Church has always believed it.)", "What does it show us? (What awaits us: the resurrection of the body.)"],
    practice=["Draw a strip with twelve months.", "Mark Mary’s feasts: January 1 (Mary, Mother of God), February 11 (Our Lady of Lourdes), March 25 (Annunciation), May 31 (Visitation), August 15 (Assumption), September 8 (Birthday of Mary), October 7 (Our Lady of the Rosary), December 8 (Immaculate Conception), December 12 (Our Lady of Guadalupe).", "Add a symbol for each.", "Circle the Assumption and write a sentence."],
    close="Pray the Hail, Holy Queen, or the Hail Mary. Sign of the Cross. Send home: “Celebrate Mary’s next feast with your family.”")),

'4-7-1': dict(
  saint="John Chrysostom: the “golden-mouthed” preacher who taught about the sacraments.",
  tryit=["Fill in the seven sacraments chart.", "Write the sign, the words and the minister.", "Say what each gives."],
  saintStory="John was born in Antioch around 347, and his mother, Anthusa, was widowed young and raised him in the faith. He studied to be a lawyer, but he became a monk and then a priest. He was such a gifted preacher that he was called “Chrysostom,” which means “golden mouth.”\nIn 398 he became the archbishop of Constantinople, the capital of the empire. He preached to rich and poor, and he spoke up against injustice, luxury and unkindness to the poor. He told the people that a poor person’s hungry belly is the altar of Christ, and that the Eucharist and care for the poor go together. Some powerful people did not like it, and he was sent into exile twice.\nJohn died in 407, still on the way to a distant place of exile. He wrote beautiful homilies on Baptism and the Eucharist. He shows that the sacraments are for a life of love.",
  script=dict(
    open="Give each child a coin. Say, “This is money. Does it have value? What gives it value? Because we agree on it. The sacraments are different. They have real power because Jesus gave them.”",
    teach=["Say: “The sacraments are seven effective signs, instituted by Christ and given to the Church, through which we receive his grace. ‘Effective’ means they actually do what they signify. In Baptism, water washes. In the Eucharist, bread becomes Christ’s Body. In Mark 16:15-16, Jesus told his disciples, ‘Go into all the world… He who believes and is baptized will be saved.’ Jesus is the one at work in every sacrament; the priest acts in his person.”", "Say: “Each sacrament has the same parts. Matter: the visible sign, such as water, oil, bread and wine. Form: the words that are said, such as ‘I baptize you…’ Minister: the person who celebrates it. And the recipient, the one who receives it with faith. When all these are present, Christ acts. The sacrament works because of what Christ does (ex opere operato), though the grace we receive depends on how open we are.”", "Say: “The seven sacraments are in three groups. The sacraments of Christian initiation: Baptism, Confirmation and Eucharist. The sacraments of healing: Reconciliation and Anointing of the Sick. The sacraments at the service of communion: Holy Orders and Matrimony. They touch every stage of life: birth, growth, nourishment, healing, mission and love.”"],
    ask=["What is a sacrament? (An effective sign instituted by Christ that gives grace.)", "What are the parts of a sacrament? (Matter, form, minister.)", "Who is really acting in each sacrament? (Christ.)"],
    practice=["Draw a chart with the columns: Sacrament, Sign (matter), Words (form), Minister, What it gives.", "Fill in each of the seven rows with the group, using the Catechism or a handout.", "For example: Baptism, water, ‘I baptize you…’, priest or deacon (anyone in an emergency), new life.", "Quiz each other on the chart."],
    close="Pray: “Jesus, thank you for the sacraments.” Sign of the Cross. Send home: “Teach your family the seven sacraments.”")),

'4-7-2': dict(
  saint="Benedict: a monk who built a rule of daily habits for life.",
  tryit=["Pick one virtue.", "Design a habit tracker for it.", "Try it for a week."],
  saintStory="Benedict was born around 480 in Nursia, Italy. His family sent him to Rome to study, but he saw so many people living badly that he left and went to live in a cave, in prayer. Other people heard about this holy young man and came to learn from him.\nBenedict started twelve small monasteries, and then a larger one at Monte Cassino. For his monks he wrote a short rule of life, a plan for each day: time for prayer, time for work and time for reading. This is why monks are known by the motto “Pray and work.” The Rule is a plan for forming good habits and living together in peace. It starts with the word “Listen.”\nHe died around 547. Benedict’s Rule has shaped monasteries for 1,500 years. He shows how virtues are built by practice, one day at a time.",
  script=dict(
    open="Hold up a basketball (or a musical instrument). Ask, “How do you get good at basketball? Do you get good by watching, or by playing every day?” Say, “Being good at loving is the same.”",
    teach=["Say: “A virtue is a good habit that helps us do what is right. A habit is something you do so often that it becomes easy. Brushing your teeth, or riding a bike. A good habit, like honesty, patience or generosity, makes it easier to choose well. Bad habits, called vices, do the opposite. Paul wrote, ‘Whatever is true, whatever is honorable, whatever is just, whatever is pure, whatever is lovely… think about these things.’ (Philippians 4:8)”", "Say: “Conscience is the inner judgment that tells us whether an act is right or wrong. We grow in virtue by practice, repeating good acts. We need prayer and the sacraments, which give grace. We learn from the saints, from our parents, and from the Church’s teaching. Like Benedict’s Rule, a plan helps: set times to pray, work, study and rest.”", "Say: “There are human virtues and divine ones. The four cardinal virtues are prudence, justice, fortitude and temperance. The three theological virtues are faith, hope and charity, which God gives us at Baptism. Other virtues, like patience, honesty and generosity, grow out of these. The more we practice, the more we become the person God made us to be.”"],
    ask=["What is a virtue? (A good habit that helps us do what is right.)", "How do we grow in virtue? (Practice, prayer, sacraments, good examples.)", "What is a vice? (A bad habit.)"],
    practice=["Each child chooses one virtue to work on: patience, honesty, generosity, courage, gratitude, kindness.", "On a card, design a habit tracker: a grid of seven days, with a small action for each day (for example, honesty: tell the truth even when it is hard).", "Add a space for a short prayer.", "Share the plan with a partner, and agree to check in next week."],
    close="Pray: “Lord, help me grow in virtue.” Sign of the Cross. Send home: “Fill in your tracker each night.”")),

'4-7-3': dict(
  saint="Paul Miki: a Jesuit who preached the Gospel from his cross and forgave his killers.",
  tryit=["Build a bridge between a commandment and a Beatitude.", "Say how they go together.", "Choose one to practice."],
  saintStory="Paul Miki was born in Japan around 1562, to a noble family. He studied with the Jesuits and became a Jesuit brother, an excellent preacher. In his time, Christianity had grown in Japan, but the ruler Hideyoshi feared it, and ordered Christians arrested.\nIn 1597, twenty-six Christians were arrested and marched about 600 miles through winter, to Nagasaki, to be executed on crosses. They were priests, brothers and lay people, including three boys, who were twelve to fourteen years old. Along the way they sang and prayed. Paul Miki, standing on his cross, preached to the crowd, saying that he was Japanese and a Christian, and that he forgave everyone who had ordered his death.\nThe martyrs of Japan show the meaning of the Beatitudes: blessed are those who are persecuted for righteousness’ sake, and blessed are the merciful. They lived what the commandments and the Beatitudes ask.",
  script=dict(
    open="Show a picture of a high-jump bar, or draw a line on the floor. Ask, “What is the minimum? What is the highest anyone can go?” Say, “The Ten Commandments are like the line you must not go below. The Beatitudes show how high we can go.”",
    teach=["Read Matthew 22:37-40. Say: “A lawyer asked Jesus, ‘Which is the greatest commandment?’ Jesus said, ‘You shall love the Lord your God with all your heart, and with all your soul, and with all your mind. This is the great and first commandment. And a second is like it: You shall love your neighbor as yourself. On these two commandments depend all the Law and the Prophets.’ All Ten Commandments are about love.”", "Say: “The Ten Commandments show us the minimum of love: do not kill, do not steal, do not lie. They protect people and our relationship with God. The Beatitudes show the heights of love: be merciful, make peace, be pure in heart, be poor in spirit. They go beyond what the law requires. Jesus said, ‘I have not come to abolish the law but to fulfill it.’ The Beatitudes fulfill the commandments.”", "Say: “For example, the commandment ‘You shall not kill’ tells us never to harm others. The Beatitude ‘Blessed are the peacemakers’ calls us to go beyond it: to actively make peace. The commandment ‘You shall not steal’ is fulfilled in ‘Blessed are the poor in spirit’: being generous and trusting God. They work together: the commandments are the foundation, and the Beatitudes are the roof.”"],
    ask=["What are the two great commandments? (Love God and love your neighbor.)", "How are the commandments and the Beatitudes different? (Commandments: the minimum; Beatitudes: the heights of love.)", "How do they fit together? (The Beatitudes fulfill the commandments.)"],
    practice=["Draw a bridge on paper. On one side write a commandment. On the other side write a Beatitude that goes with it.", "Examples: “You shall not kill” and “Blessed are the peacemakers”; “You shall not steal” and “Blessed are the poor in spirit”; “You shall not lie” and “Blessed are the pure in heart.”", "On the bridge, write one action that brings the two together.", "Share your bridge, and choose one to practice."],
    close="Pray: “Jesus, help us love as you love.” Sign of the Cross. Send home: “Choose a Beatitude to live this week.”")),

'4-8-1': dict(
  saint="Teresa of Ávila: she wrote about the Our Father line by line.",
  tryit=["Write each petition in your own words.", "Fold the cards into a booklet.", "Pray it slowly."],
  saintStory="Teresa of Ávila was born in 1515 in Spain. When she was a teenager, she became a Carmelite nun. For about twenty years she found it hard to pray and was easily distracted. But then, after a deep experience in front of a statue of the suffering Christ, she changed. She began to pray with her whole heart.\nTeresa started a reform of the Carmelites, with small convents of sisters who lived simply and prayed daily. In 1566 she wrote a book for her sisters called The Way of Perfection. A large part of it is about the Our Father: she goes through it line by line, explaining each petition. She wrote that when we say “Our Father,” we speak to the Father with Jesus, who is right beside us.\nShe is a Doctor of the Church. Teresa shows how a simple prayer can become a conversation with God that lasts a lifetime.",
  script=dict(
    open="Ask the class to say the Our Father together quickly. Then ask, “Did you think about the words? It’s easy to say a prayer fast, and miss what it says. Let’s unpack it.”",
    teach=["Say: “The Church Father Tertullian called the Our Father ‘the summary of the whole Gospel.’ Jesus gave it to us in Matthew 6:9-13. It starts by naming God as ‘Our Father,’ which means we pray together, as a family. We say ‘Our’ because we pray with others. ‘Who art in heaven’ does not mean far away, but that God is above all.”", "Say: “The first three petitions are about God. ‘Hallowed be thy name’: may your name be honored. ‘Thy kingdom come’: may your reign come on earth. ‘Thy will be done on earth as it is in heaven’: may everyone do what you want. The next four are about us. ‘Give us this day our daily bread’: we ask for what we need, both bread and the Eucharist. ‘And forgive us our trespasses, as we forgive those who trespass against us’: we ask for forgiveness and promise to forgive. ‘And lead us not into temptation’: help us avoid temptation and to resist it. ‘But deliver us from evil’: protect us from the evil one, and from all evil.”", "Say: “At Mass the priest adds, ‘Deliver us, Lord, from every evil.’ And we say, ‘For the kingdom, the power and the glory are yours, now and forever.’ That is called the doxology. The Amen at the end means ‘so be it.’ Praying the Our Father slowly, thinking about the words, can be a lifelong prayer.”"],
    ask=["What are the first three petitions about? (God: his name, kingdom and will.)", "What are the last four about? (Us: bread, forgiveness, help in temptation, protection from evil.)", "What does ‘Our’ show? (We pray together as a family.)"],
    practice=["Give each child seven cards, one for each petition.", "On the front, write the petition from the Our Father. On the back, write it in their own words and draw a picture. Example: “Give us this day our daily bread”: “God, please give me and my family what we need today.”", "Fold them into a small booklet, or staple them.", "Pray the Our Father together, using the booklets, slowly, one petition at a time."],
    close="Pray the Our Father very slowly. Sign of the Cross. Send home: “Pray your booklet with someone at home.”")),

'4-8-2': dict(
  saint="John of the Cross: a poet and monk who showed how to rest in God.",
  tryit=["Build a “prayer menu.”", "Write an example for each of the five forms.", "Try one."],
  saintStory="Juan de Yepes was born in Spain in 1542, into a poor family. His father died when he was a little boy. He became a Carmelite friar, and took the name John of the Cross. He worked with Teresa of Ávila to reform the Carmelites.\nSome of the friars didn’t like the reform. In 1577, they kidnapped John and locked him in a tiny cell in Toledo, where he was beaten and given little to eat. For nine months he lived in darkness. There, he composed some of the most beautiful poems in the Spanish language. He escaped one night by squeezing out of a window and climbing down a rope. He was weak, but he kept writing.\nJohn of the Cross wrote about the “dark night,” when God seems far, and about resting in God’s love. He is a Doctor of the Church. He shows that prayer can also be silence, trust and waiting.",
  script=dict(
    open="Hand out a mock restaurant menu with five sections but no food names. Ask, “What would you order? Today we have a menu of ways to talk to God.”",
    teach=["Say: “Prayer is the raising of the mind and heart to God. The Church teaches five forms of prayer. Blessing and adoration: we bless God and adore him as our Creator and Lord. Petition: we ask God for what we need, and for forgiveness. Intercession: we ask for others. Thanksgiving: we thank God for all he has given. Praise: we praise God just for who he is. For example, Psalm 63:1: ‘O God, you are my God, I seek you, my soul thirsts for you.’”", "Say: “Prayer is also expressed in three ways. Vocal prayer uses words, aloud or silently, such as the Our Father or a prayer we make up. Meditation is thoughtful prayer: we think about a verse of the Bible or a scene from the Gospels, and ask, ‘What is God saying to me?’ Contemplation is a simple loving look at God. It is silent, resting in him, like looking at a friend without needing words. St. Teresa of Ávila called it ‘a close sharing between friends.’”", "Say: “The Psalms are the prayer book of the Church. Every kind of prayer is in them: praise, thanks, sorrow, asking for help. Jesus prayed the Psalms. The Church prays them every day in the Liturgy of the Hours. A method of prayer called Lectio Divina means ‘divine reading’: read a short Scripture passage slowly, meditate on it, pray in response, and rest in silence. Prayer is a gift, and we learn it by praying.”"],
    ask=["Name the five forms of prayer. (Blessing/adoration, petition, intercession, thanksgiving, praise.)", "What are the three expressions? (Vocal, meditation, contemplation.)", "What is Lectio Divina? (Prayerful reading of Scripture.)"],
    practice=["Give each child a “prayer menu” paper with five sections.", "For each form of prayer, they write a short example: “Thank you for my family,” “Please help my friend who is sick,” “You are great, God.”", "Try Lectio Divina together with Psalm 63:1-8: read it slowly twice, share one word, then keep silence for a minute.", "Each child picks an item from the menu to pray this week."],
    close="Pray the Jesus Prayer slowly: “Lord Jesus Christ, Son of God, have mercy on me.” Sign of the Cross. Send home: “Choose a way of praying from your menu each day.”")),

'4-8-3': dict(
  saint="Stanislaus Kostka: a teenage student who loved the Eucharist and walked hundreds of miles to follow his calling.",
  tryit=["Fold the strip into the parts of Mass.", "Write what happens in each part.", "Describe how Jesus meets us."],
  saintStory="Stanislaus Kostka was born in Poland in 1550, to a noble family. At fourteen he went with his older brother to a school in Vienna. His brother was hard on him for being so serious about prayer and for attending Mass so often, and he often scolded him for it.\nWhen he fell dangerously ill, he said that Mary came to him, with the child Jesus, and that he was healed. He wanted to join the Jesuits, but his father forbade it. So, at seventeen, Stanislaus slipped out of Vienna in disguise and walked hundreds of miles to Germany and then to Rome, where he was accepted by the Jesuit leader.\nHe lived as a novice for less than a year, praying and working with great joy. He died in 1568, at only seventeen. He is the patron of students. Stanislaus shows how much a young person can love Jesus in the Eucharist.",
  script=dict(
    open="Hand each child a long strip of paper. Say, “This is a path. We will walk it step by step, the way we walk through Mass. Every step is for something.”",
    teach=["Say: “The Church’s year unfolds the whole story of Jesus. We have Advent, Christmas, Lent, Easter, Pentecost and Ordinary Time. Each season lets us live a part of the mystery. And the heart of it all is the Mass. At Mass, Jesus is present, in his word and in the Eucharist. The Mass is the source and summit of our Christian life.”", "Say: “The Mass has two main parts. First, the Liturgy of the Word. We gather and say sorry. We listen to readings from the Old Testament, a psalm, the New Testament and the Gospel. The priest gives a homily. We say the Creed and pray for the world. Second, the Liturgy of the Eucharist. The gifts of bread and wine are brought up. The priest says the Eucharistic Prayer. We pray the Our Father, share a sign of peace, and receive Communion. Then we are sent out: ‘Go in peace, glorifying the Lord by your life.’”", "Read Luke 24:13-35 in summary. Say: “On the road to Emmaus, Jesus walked with two sad disciples. He explained the Scriptures, and their hearts burned. Then at supper, he broke the bread, and they recognized him. This is the shape of the Mass: Jesus speaks to us in the Word, and Jesus feeds us in the Eucharist. We meet him in both. Then, like the disciples, we go out to tell others.”"],
    ask=["What are the two main parts of the Mass? (The Liturgy of the Word and the Liturgy of the Eucharist.)", "Where do we meet Jesus at Mass? (In his word and in the Eucharist.)", "What happened on the road to Emmaus? (Jesus explained the Scriptures and broke bread.)"],
    practice=["Fold a long strip of paper into eight parts: Introductory Rites; First Reading; Psalm; Second Reading; Gospel and Homily; Eucharistic Prayer; Communion; Concluding Rites.", "Write the name of each part on the front. On the back, write what happens, and draw a symbol.", "Mark each part where Jesus meets us (the Word, the Eucharist).", "Walk through the strip with a partner and explain it."],
    close="Pray the prayer: “Lord, I am not worthy that you should enter under my roof.” Sign of the Cross. Send home: “Follow the parts of Mass next Sunday.”")),

'4-9-1': dict(
  saint="John Bosco: a priest who trusted God to provide for his boys.",
  tryit=["Make two posters.", "“Things only God can do” and “How God uses his power to love.”", "Find an example of each in the Bible."],
  saintStory="John Bosco was born in 1815 in a poor farming family in northern Italy. His father died when he was two, and his mother, Margaret, raised him. As a boy he learned to do tricks like juggling and walking on a tightrope, to attract a crowd of friends, and then he would say the Rosary with them or repeat the sermon he had heard at Mass.\nHe became a priest and worked in the city of Turin, where many boys had no home or school. He gathered them in an “oratory,” and gave them food, lessons and prayer, but he had little money. Again and again, he trusted God to provide, and what he needed always seemed to come. He started schools and workshops, and a group of priests, the Salesians, to carry on the work.\nBosco died in 1888, having helped thousands of boys. He shows that God is almighty, and that his power is used with love.",
  script=dict(
    open="Show a picture of a very powerful thing (an avalanche or a rocket) and a picture of a mother holding a baby. Ask, “Which one shows more power? Which one shows more love? Is it possible to have both?” Say, “That is what we say about God.”",
    teach=["Say: “The Creed begins, ‘I believe in God, the Father almighty.’ Almighty means that God can do anything. When Gabriel told Mary that Elizabeth was going to have a child, he said, ‘Nothing is impossible with God.’ (Luke 1:37) God made everything out of nothing. He controls nature. He raises the dead. But God never uses his power to hurt or to show off. His power is always used with love and with justice.”", "Say: “God is our Father. A father cares for his children. Jesus said, ‘Do not be anxious about your life… Look at the birds of the air… your heavenly Father feeds them. Are you not of more value than they?’ (Matthew 6:25-34) God knows each of us and loves us. He is Father of all things he has made. We can trust him.”", "Say: “God watches over everything he has made. This is called providence. God guides the whole world toward its goal, and he cares for each person. Sometimes we do not understand why bad things happen. God allows evil in the world, because he gave us free will, and he can bring good out of it. But his love is never absent. We believe that his plan is good, and we pray, ‘Thy will be done.’”"],
    ask=["What does ‘almighty’ mean? (God can do anything.)", "What is providence? (God’s loving care and guidance over everything.)", "How is God’s power different from earthly power? (It is always used with love.)"],
    practice=["Make two posters. On the first: “Things only God can do” (create from nothing, forgive sins, raise the dead, know everything). On the second: “How God uses his power to love” (feeds the birds, healed the sick, saved us, calms the storm).", "For each, find a Bible example (Genesis 1, Mark 2:5-12, John 11, Mark 4:35-41).", "Add one example from your own life of God’s care.", "Share posters in groups."],
    close="Pray the Our Father together. Sign of the Cross. Send home: “Tell your family a time God took care of you.”")),

'4-9-2': dict(
  saint="Melito of Sardis: an early bishop who preached an Easter sermon about Christ’s victory.",
  tryit=["Mark Holy Saturday on the timeline.", "Draw the open doors of the tomb.", "Draw the people who waited."],
  saintStory="Melito was bishop of Sardis, a city in what is now Turkey, around the year 170. We know little about his life, but he was highly respected, and traveled to the Holy Land to learn about the books of the Old Testament. He wrote a number of works.\nOne of them survives, a homily on Easter, called On Pascha. Melito describes how Jesus, the Lamb, was sacrificed for us, and how, in his death, he conquered death. He says that Christ is the one who “rescued us from slavery to freedom, from darkness to light, from death to life.” The early Church loved this idea: when Jesus died, his soul descended to the place of the dead, and he set the just free.\nMelito reminds us of how ancient our faith is. The words “He descended to the dead” belong to the Creed, and they tell us what Jesus did on Holy Saturday.",
  script=dict(
    open="Hold a quiet moment: turn off the lights and say nothing for thirty seconds. Then whisper, “What was it like for the disciples on Saturday? Everything seemed to have ended. But something was happening that they could not see.”",
    teach=["Read Matthew 27:57-66 in summary. Say: “Joseph of Arimathea laid Jesus’ body in his own tomb. A large stone was rolled across the door. The next day, which was the Sabbath, the chief priests and the Pharisees asked Pilate to put a guard on the tomb. That day, Holy Saturday, is the quietest day of the year. The Church does not celebrate Mass. The altar is bare. We wait.”", "Say: “The Apostles’ Creed says, ‘He descended into hell’ or ‘He descended to the dead.’ It does not mean the hell of the damned. It means the place of the dead, sometimes called Sheol or Hades. Jesus really died: his soul separated from his body. His body lay in the tomb. His soul went to the place of the dead, where the holy ones waited, Adam and Eve, Abraham, Moses, David, and all those who had lived faithfully before Christ. 1 Peter 3:18-20 says that Christ ‘went and proclaimed to the spirits in prison.’”", "Say: “There he announced the good news and opened the gates of heaven to the just. In icons of the Resurrection in the Eastern Church, Jesus is shown pulling Adam and Eve out of their graves. This is a picture of what he did. Jesus’ death is a victory, not a defeat. Holy Saturday teaches us to wait in hope, trusting that something is happening in the silence.”"],
    ask=["What does ‘He descended to the dead’ mean? (Jesus’ soul went to the place of the dead to free the holy ones.)", "Who did Jesus free? (Adam and Eve and all the just who waited.)", "What is Holy Saturday like in the Church? (Quiet, no Mass, waiting.)"],
    practice=["Draw a timeline from Good Friday to Easter, with three parts: Friday, Saturday, Sunday.", "On Saturday, draw the tomb with the doors open, and Jesus reaching out to Adam, Eve and the holy people.", "Write a caption: “Jesus set the just free.”", "Share and talk about waiting in hope."],
    close="Pray quietly: “Jesus, we wait in hope.” Sign of the Cross. Send home: “Spend a few minutes in quiet on Holy Saturday.”")),

'4-9-3': dict(
  saint="Joseph: the quiet man whose whole life was a yes to God.",
  tryit=["Say a prayer with a loud Amen.", "Make an AMEN banner.", "Choose a prayer to say every day."],
  saintStory="Joseph is the quietest person in the Gospels. Not one word he said is recorded. But everything he did was a yes to God.\nWhen Joseph found that Mary was expecting a child, he was going to quietly step aside. Then an angel told him in a dream, “Do not be afraid to take Mary as your wife.” Joseph got up and did what the angel said. Later, in other dreams, he was told to take the child to Egypt, and then to return to Nazareth. Each time, he obeyed right away. He worked as a carpenter to support his family.\nJoseph is the patron of the Universal Church, of fathers, and of workers. His life is like a long “Amen.” He shows that saying yes to God, quietly and faithfully, is the greatest thing we can do.",
  script=dict(
    open="Say a series of statements, some true and some false. After each one, the children respond, “Amen!” if it is true. For example: “God is good.” “God made the world.” “The sky is made of cheese.” Say, “‘Amen’ means ‘so be it, I agree.’ Today we will see why it matters.”",
    teach=["Say: “‘Amen’ is a Hebrew word that means ‘so be it,’ ‘it is true,’ and ‘I believe it.’ We say it at the end of the Creed and at the end of nearly every prayer. We say it when we receive Communion: the priest says, ‘The Body of Christ,’ and we say, ‘Amen.’ We are saying, ‘Yes, I believe this is Jesus.’”", "Read Revelation 3:14 and 2 Corinthians 1:20. Say: “Jesus is called ‘the Amen, the faithful and true witness.’ In him, all of God’s promises are ‘Yes.’ When we say ‘Amen’ to God, we say yes to what he has said, to what he has promised and to what he asks of us. Mary’s ‘let it be done to me’ was an Amen. Joseph’s obedience was an Amen.”", "Say: “The Creed ends with the words ‘life everlasting. Amen.’ This is our hope: that we will live with God forever. We pray with confidence. We say Amen aloud, not as a mumble. It is a sign that we mean what we say, and that we belong to the Church’s prayer. And when we say it with our lives, by trusting God and doing what he asks, we are saying Amen with our whole selves.”"],
    ask=["What does ‘Amen’ mean? (So be it, I believe.)", "When do we say Amen? (End of prayers, the Creed, at Communion.)", "How can we say Amen with our lives? (By trusting God and doing what he asks.)"],
    practice=["Say a short prayer together, for example the Glory Be, and shout a loud, joyful “Amen!”", "Give each child a paper strip to make a banner with the word AMEN in large decorated letters.", "Under it, write a prayer you will say every day this week (Our Father, Hail Mary, Creed).", "Hang the banners in a row."],
    close="Pray the Creed together, ending with a strong “Amen!” Sign of the Cross. Send home: “Say Amen with meaning at Mass.”")),
}
